        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric smokers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you want simple, steady smoking with low running costs. They are excellent for fish, jerky, cheese and pulled pork. They produce milder flavor and less bark than charcoal or wood smokers, so serious barbecue fans may prefer a pellet or charcoal model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric smoker for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Masterbuilt 30-Inch Digital is the easiest place to start: it has a digital controller, a side wood chip loader and enough space for most family cooks at a modest price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use an electric smoker in the winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but expect longer cooks. Fixed-wattage elements struggle to hold temperature in cold wind. An insulated cover, a windbreak and keeping the door closed help, and higher-wattage models recover faster.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you soak wood chips for an electric smoker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Dry chips start smoking sooner and more cleanly. Soaked chips steam first and can stop smoking at low temperatures. Add dry chips in small amounts throughout the cook.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an electric smoker on a covered porch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the space is fully open to the air with the manufacturer's clearances. Never use it in an enclosed porch, garage or indoors, because smoldering wood produces carbon monoxide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much electricity does an electric smoker use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical 750- to 800-watt smoker uses a few kilowatt-hours over an eight-hour cook, because the element cycles on and off. Higher-wattage models like the Cuisinart COS-330 draw more current while heating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between an electric smoker and a pellet grill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An electric smoker uses an electric element for heat and smolders chips for smoke. A pellet grill burns wood pellets for both heat and smoke, giving more flavor and higher temperatures. Both need power.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

